-- title:
--   Mean reward $\mu(x,\theta)$ of an action under a parameter
-- statement:
--   Let $w_1,w_2,\dots$ be the disturbances of the model, $g:\mathcal X\times\Theta\times W\to Y$ the outcome function and $r:Y\to\mathbb R$ the reward. The mean reward of action $x$ under the parameter value $\vartheta$ is
--
--   $$\mu(x,\vartheta)=\int_W r\big(g(x,\vartheta,v)\big)\,\mathrm d\mathcal L(w_1)(v),$$
--
--   the expected reward of $x$ when the parameter equals $\vartheta$, the expectation being over the disturbance. When the disturbances are i.i.d. and independent of $\theta$, $\omega\mapsto\mu(x,\theta(\omega))$ is a version of the paper's $\mu(x,\theta)=\mathbb E[r(g(x,\theta,w_t))\mid\theta]$ for every period $t$.
--
--   This is the quantity Thompson sampling maximizes in the sampled model and in terms of which regret is measured.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Periods are 1-based, so the law of $w_1$ is used. No integrability of $v\mapsto r(g(x,\vartheta,v))$ is assumed; where it fails the paper's $\mu$ is undefined and the Bochner integral returns $0$.
-- source:
--   Russo, Van Roy, Kazerouni, Osband, Wen, A Tutorial on Thompson Sampling, Found. Trends Mach. Learn. 11(1) (2018), doi:10.1561/2200000070, §8.1.2 Problem Formulation, pp. 70–71 (PDF pp. 73–74), definition of µ(x, θ)

import Mathlib
open MeasureTheory

namespace ThompsonTutorial.Regret

/-- **Mean reward `µ(x, θ)`.**
Russo, Van Roy, Kazerouni, Osband, Wen, *A Tutorial on Thompson Sampling*, Found. Trends Mach.
Learn. 11(1) (2018), §8.1.2 "Problem Formulation", pp. 70–71 (PDF pp. 73–74): "Let
µ(x, θ) = E[r(g(x, θ, wt)) | θ] denote the expected reward generated by the action x under the
parameter θ, where this expectation is taken over the disturbance wt."

`meanReward P w g r x ϑ = ∫ r(g(x, ϑ, v)) d(law of w₁)(v)`: the expected reward of action `x` when
the parameter takes the value `ϑ`, the expectation being over the disturbance. Since the
disturbances `w_t` are identically distributed and independent of `θ` (see `IsBayesModel`),
`ω ↦ meanReward P w g r x (θ ω)` is a version of `E[r(g(x, θ, w_t)) | θ]` for every period `t ≥ 1`.

**Formalization Note.** Periods are 1-based, so the law of the first disturbance `w 1` is used
(`w 0` is never used). No integrability of `v ↦ r(g(x, ϑ, v))` is assumed: where it fails the
paper's `µ` is undefined and the Bochner integral is `0`. -/
noncomputable def meanReward {Ω Θ X Y W : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] [MeasurableSpace W]
    (P : Measure Ω) (w : ℕ → Ω → W) (g : X → Θ → W → Y) (r : Y → ℝ) (x : X) (ϑ : Θ) : ℝ :=
  ∫ v, r (g x ϑ v) ∂(P.map (w 1))

end ThompsonTutorial.Regret


